{"name":"Toxic Slime","hex":"#43e85f","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"toxic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a chemical nature that is harmful to health or lethal if consumed or otherwise entering into the body in sufficient quantities.","Appearing grossly unwell; characterised by serious, potentially life-threatening compromise in the respiratory, circulatory or other body systems."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/toxic"},{"term":"slime","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Soft, moist earth or clay, having an adhesive quality; viscous mud; any substance of a dirty nature, that is moist, soft, and adhesive; bitumen; mud containing metallic ore, obtained in the preparatory dressing.","Any mucilaginous substance; or a mucus-like substance which exudes from the bodies of certain animals, such as snails or slugs."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To coat with slime.","To besmirch or disparage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/slime"}],"entries":[{"title":"Slime (toy)","blocks":[{"text":"Slime is a toy product manufactured by Mattel, sold in a plastic trash can and introduced in February 1976. It consists of a non-toxic viscous, squishy and oozy green or other color material made primarily from guar gum. Different variations of Slime have been released over the years, including Slime containing rubber insects, eyeballs, and worms."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Pouring_Slime.JPG/330px-Pouring_Slime.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slime_(toy)"},{"title":"Slime (homemade toy)","blocks":[{"text":"Slime is a homemade toy typically created using a combination of water, glue, and borax. Videos of people playing with slime became popular on social media in the mid-2010s, which made it an international trend."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Slime_02471_Nevit.jpg/330px-Slime_02471_Nevit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slime_(homemade_toy)"},{"title":"Toxicity","blocks":[{"text":"Toxicity is the degree to which a chemical substance or a particular mixture of substances can damage an organism. Toxicity can refer to the effect on a whole organism, such as an animal, bacterium, or plant, as well as the effect on a substructure of the organism, such as a cell (cytotoxicity) or an organ such as the liver (hepatotoxicity). Sometimes the word is more or less synonymous with poisoning in everyday usage."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Skull_and_Crossbones.svg/330px-Skull_and_Crossbones.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toxicity"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/SlimeAnionicBorate.png/500px-SlimeAnionicBorate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/SlimeAnionicBorate.png/500px-SlimeAnionicBorate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Structure for borate ester that comprises crosslinking in \"slime\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASlimeAnionicBorate.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Two_girls_holding_homemade_slime.jpg/500px-Two_girls_holding_homemade_slime.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Two_girls_holding_homemade_slime.jpg/1280px-Two_girls_holding_homemade_slime.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Two_girls_holding_homemade_slime.jpg/500px-Two_girls_holding_homemade_slime.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Two young girls holding up slime made using glue, baking soda, shaving cream, food coloring, and contact lens solution","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATwo_girls_holding_homemade_slime.jpg"}],"sounds":{"toxic":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cd/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-toxic.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/c/cd/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-toxic.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-toxic.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/c/cd/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-toxic.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-toxic.wav.ogg"},{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d4/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Qwertygiy-toxic.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/d/d4/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Qwertygiy-toxic.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Qwertygiy-toxic.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/d/d4/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Qwertygiy-toxic.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Qwertygiy-toxic.wav.ogg"}],"slime":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/44/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-slime.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/4/44/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-slime.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-slime.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/4/44/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-slime.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-slime.wav.ogg"},{"type":"audio/wav